th eugene  kleiner's father was getting ready to  retire so he passed the letter to a  young associate arthur rock as it  happened rock was the perfect person to  receive the rebels proposal two years  earlier he'd worked on a funding package  for general transistor the first  independent manufacturer of germanium  semiconductors so he knew about the  promise of this emerging technology and  he also knew about william shockley's  reputation to rock the fact that these  eight guys were willing to quit working  with god was a sign of their strong  character he was intrigued he wrote back  to kleiner and before long was meeting  him and his colleagues for dinner in san  francisco rock understood that the eight  rebels wanted to continue working  together so he suggested something which  even though it seems so obvious today  had never occurred to them the way you  do this is you start your own company  that's how the rebels could work  independently what's more as founders  they would capture the true value of  their technical skills the eight  scientists were stunned the thought of  starting their own company had never  occurred to them it simply wasn't done  in the conformist 1950s  raising the money to do so wasn't  straightforward the idea of raising  money for these young scientists who  although they were brilliant had no real  profile of their own was a hard sell at  the time after approaching 35 potential  investors rock had failed to raise a  single cent then a colleague of his  suggested he approached sherman  fairchild a self-described putterer who  had inherited a fortune and was  interested in science fairchild loved  what the rebels were up to so he put in  the money they needed to start their own  company and they called it fairchild  semiconductor shockley was furious he  called the departure of the young  scientists a betrayal and predicted  their new venture would be a failure  there were many reasons he was wrong but  let's just focus on one let's fast  forward to 1965 and zoom in on a single  member of the team gordon moore moore  who by then was making a name for  himself as the director of r d at  fairchild was asked to contribute to the  35th anniversary of electronics magazine  by making a prediction about the future  of the semiconductor industry his  contribution was a short article called  cramming more components onto integrated  circuits in the paper moore observed  that the number of components in a dense  integrated circuit had doubled  approximately every year and speculated  that it would continue to do so for at  least the next 10 years essentially more  components on an integrated circuit  meant more computing power by 1975  exactly 10 years later moore had seen  enough progress in the field to revise  his forecast to say that the number of  components would double every two years  his predictions weren't based on  empirical evidence moore even called his  original forecast a wild extrapolation  but his forecast proved so accurate that  before long it was dubbed moore's law  calling it a law though didn't make it  one moore didn't actually create a  technology which allowed the number of  transistors on a circuit to double every  two years instead the semiconductor  industry adopted this as its goal  companies used moore's insight to guide  their long-term planning and set targets  for research and development almost  every advancement in modern computing  and electronics is tied to moore's law  gordon moore brought the future of  computing and electronics into clear  view he was about to bring his own  future into view as well in 1968 he and  his fellow traitorous 8 member robert  noyce decided to start a new company to  harness the innovations in  miniaturization and processing power  which the transistor had made possible  their impeccable credentials convinced  arthur rock to arrange 2.5 million  dollars to help get the new company off  the ground neuse and moore called their  company nm electronics after their last  initials but before long they changed  its name to the one it still bears today  intel intel is a truly pioneering  company in 1971 the year it went public  it created the world's first commercial  microprocessor chip its big investments  in new designs like the pentium  microprocessor in the 1990s accelerated  the rise of pcs as a viable industry it  developed the x86 family of  microprocessors which still forms the  basis of most pcs laptops and game  consoles in the run up to the 40th  anniversary of moore's article in  electronics intel put out a call for an  original issue of the magazine they  didn't have a copy and neither did  gordon moore they eventually found one  thanks to an engineer from the uk named  david clark clark started collecting  copies of electronics and other trade  magazines in the 1970s when his local  library started throwing them out  despite his wife's pleas he held on to  them for decades he ended up finding the  issue of electronics with moore's famous  article under his floorboards when he  was asked what he would do with the ten  thousand dollar reward clark said that  he and his wife intended to use it to  pay for their daughter's wedding even  when he didn't mean to gordon moore was  creating value the second law that  explained silicon valley was also born  from ambition and ego let's talk about a  guy named robert metcalf in 1973 while  working at xerox park the division of  xerox tasked with creating  computer-related products and hardware  systems metcalf and his colleague david  boggs co-founded ethernet they were  inspired by aloha net a pioneering  computer networking system developed at  the university of hawaii metcalfe grew  frustrated with xerox who despite filing  a patent application for ethernet in  1975 showed no interest in building a  business from the technology so in june  of 1979 he quit and founded 3com in his  apartment in palo alto based on his  belief that ethernet would emerge as 