ice in a liquid market it  was the perfect positive feedback loop  what's more because ebay owned the  network it captured all the value  created by its growth we can see  metcalf's law in places other than the  internet tesla's self-driving technology  also benefits from network effects you  might think that having 100 self-driving  teslas on the road is just 100 times as  valuable as having won but that's not  quite right tesla is betting on  self-driving technology becoming  dominant so it's important for them to  think ahead to a future where  self-driving technology is dominant by  taking the time today to learn how their  cars react when interacting with other  autonomous vehicles it's metcalfe's law  in action metcalfe's law isn't always  precisely correct but it is a great way  to understand why every type of tech  company endlessly chases new users and  subscribers and why the companies that  succeed capture huge markets and make  massive profits the third and final law  that explains silicon valley wasn't  actually discovered in the valley but  that's where it's had its most powerful  impact this law is often referred to as  the pareto principle after the italian  economist wilfred paredo you might know  it as the 80 20 rule meaning that  roughly 80 percent of an outcome results  from 20 of the inputs but it's best  known as the power law and it's the  final step to understanding modern tech  company valuations let's go back to a  man i mentioned when discussing moore's  law arthur rock i said that arthur rock  helped gordon moore and robert noyce  found intel i kind of buried the lead  though because in any honest history of  the venture capital industry arthur rock  is one of the main characters his  history of picking winners is ridiculous  in 1957 when the traitorous 8 left  shockley semiconductor laboratory rock  helped them find a place to go davis and  rock the firm he co-founded with his  colleague thomas davis jr in 1961 was  one of the biggest and most successful  early vc firms that same year he put up  280 000 to help start scientific data  systems eight years later that company  sold for 950 million dollars he was so  critical in the early days of intel he's  often given equal billing with more and  noise as a co-founder in 1978 rock  invested 57 400 in a fledgling personal  computer company with a whimsical yet  evocative name apple  the power law underpinned rock's  approach to investing for decades in a  speech in san francisco in 1962 he  explained that venture portfolios need  grand slams to average out the goofs  it's actually a great description of how  the vc industry has operated since its  inception consider this in early 2021 y  combinator's list of top companies  showed that about 5 of the more than 2  500 companies they had invested in were  responsible for generating a huge  majority of total returns the top  generated three-quarters of the returns  just three companies airbnb doordash and  stripe represented more than half of  yc's top company's portfolio value alone  bill gurley who's a general partner at  benchmark one of the top vc firms today  said it well vc is not even a home run  business it's a grand slam business but  why do such a small number of companies  consistently generate such a huge share  of portfolio returns you can see the  power law in action in other industries  but it's more prevalent in tech because  tech companies are themselves based upon  technologies that progress at  exponential rates arthur rock understood  this completely when co-founding davis  and rock he insisted that they would  only invest in ambitious high-growth  companies whose value might jump at  least ten-fold in five to seven years  most startups will fail they reasoned  sometimes it's because the technology  they rely on isn't mature yet other  times it's because they fail to  establish the use case for their product  sometimes it's just plain bad luck  therefore the winners would need to win  big enough to make a success of the  entire portfolio as davis put it trying  to play it safe in small companies is to  my mind self-defeating by realizing that  paradoxically the best way to manage  investment risk was to embrace it they  recognized the fundamental insight of  the power law if just a handful of  outliers hit grand slams and dominate  entire markets or even create new ones  from nothing then you've gotta swing for  the fences vcs don't always succeed  critics love to focus on what arthur  rock called the goofs but then most  companies don't succeed either in a  random group of 100 tech companies 90  will probably fail eight or nine of them  will deliver decent returns but because  of the power law in combination with  moore's law and metcalfe's law one or  two will probably generate insane  returns that's what explains why google  makes more profit than the entire global  airline industry and it's what vcs are  looking for when they back speculative  innovations like flying cars artificial  intelligence or elon's plan to fly a  rocket to mars their job is to look  beyond the horizon to a future that has  yet to be built understanding how  moore's law metcalfe's law and the power  law interact is key to understanding  modern tech companies one of the latest  unicorn startups to capitalize on  metcalfe's law is channel sponsor  masterworks dot io see traditionally you  had to be extremely wealthy to build a  proper portfolio of fine art but  masterworks changes that by allowing you  to invest in world famous artworks from  banksy monet and other iconic artists  for just a fraction of what these pieces  typically cost this is important because  contemporary art prices outpace the s p  500 total return by 164 percent from  1995 to 2021. the entire category has  historically required millions of  dollars to access but masterworks has a  secondary market where you can trade  shares in fine art just like buying a  stock on a trading app see masterworks  is a network effects business the more  people that use the service the more  valuable it becomes because the  contemporary art market they provide  becomes more liquid getting set up with  masterworks is extremely easy you just  create an account on their site browse  their artwork and then start  diversifying your portfolio with one of  the most stable assets around i like  masterworks because it unlocks an  entirely new market and it actually  teaches me something in the process so  go click the link in the description to  skip the wait list and help out the  channel and now that you understand the  three laws that explain modern tech  companies watch my video about the  amazing history of silicon valley next  thanks for watching
 

